<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-5-static-instance">
  <title>Static vs. Instance Members</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-5-1-methods">
    <title>Instance Methods vs. Static Methods</title>

    <p>
      You might be wondering what happened to the <c>static</c> keyword that we placed in 
      front of every method in Chapters 2 through 5! Let's review the difference between 
      <c>static</c> and instance methods from Chapter 5:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Static Methods:</strong> These methods are part of a class. You do not create 
          an object to use a static method<mdash/>you call the method directly on the class itself. 
          For example, <c>Double.parseDouble()</c> and <c>Math.sqrt()</c> are called directly on 
          the <c>Double</c> and <c>Math</c> classes (not on instances of those classes), as well 
          as methods we wrote in Chapter 5, such as <c>AqSummaryMethod.getRecommendation()</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Instance Methods:</strong> These are methods that operate on an individual 
          object's data (an object is also called an <em>instance</em>). You must first create 
          an object instance using <c>new</c> before calling methods on that instance. For 
          example, methods on a <c>Scanner</c> (<c>nextInt()</c>, <c>nextLine()</c>), and on a 
          <c>File</c> (<c>exists()</c>), and now you have seen programmer-defined instance methods, 
          such as <c>getX()</c> and <c>translate()</c> on <c>Point</c>, and <c>drive()</c> and 
          <c>needsCharging()</c> on <c>ElectricVehicle</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      You now have the tools to understand these differences in more depth. An instance method 
      operates with or on the data of an instance. It can give different responses every time 
      it is invoked because the response is based on the current state of the object on which 
      the method is invoked:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>"hello".length()</c> returns <c>5</c>, but <c>"".length()</c> returns <c>0</c>. 
          The <c>length()</c> method is executed each time, but the data it operates on is 
          different (<c>"hello"</c> versus <c>""</c>).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          We saw <c>p1.getX()</c> and <c>p2.getX()</c> return two different values (<c>3.0</c> 
          and <c>5.0</c>, respectively), because the internal <c>x</c> coordinate for each 
          <c>Point</c> object was different.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>cruiser.drive(100.0)</c> and <c>commuter.drive(100.0)</c> will not return anything, 
          but each will change the internal state of the <c>cruiser</c> and <c>commuter</c> 
          objects based on the values in their respective <c>milesPerKwh</c> fields. After the 
          methods execute, <c>commuter</c> will hold a <c>currentChargeKwh</c> of <c>40.0</c>, 
          <c>cruiser</c> will hold a <c>currentChargeKwh</c> of <c>51.759</c>, and both will 
          have an <c>odometerMiles</c> reading of <c>100.0</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If we invoke <c>cruiser.drive(100.0)</c> and <c>commuter.drive(100.0)</c> a second time, 
          the result is different because each object starts from a different internal state. 
          The <c>commuter</c> charge drops to <c>15.0</c>, <c>cruiser</c> drops to <c>26.118</c>, 
          and both advance to <c>200.0</c> on their odometers.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Pure utility static methods, like the methods in the <c>Math</c> class, are stateless: 
      given the same arguments, they will always return the exact same result. <c>Math.sqrt(25.0)</c>, 
      for example, will always evaluate to <c>5.0</c>. There is no internal data that changes the 
      way the <c>sqrt()</c> method operates, and there shouldn't be!
    </p>

    <p>
      Static methods that are library methods, like the <c>Math</c> methods, offer generic 
      functionality that can be used across many programming problems. Static methods are also 
      helper methods that can be invoked from another static method, like the methods we wrote 
      in Chapter 5.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <c>main</c> method must be <c>static</c> because it is where program execution begins, 
      before any objects have been created. Any helper methods that the <c>main</c> method calls 
      directly must also be <c>static</c>, unless they are invoked on an instantiated object.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-5-2-variables">
    <title>Instance Variables vs. Static Variables</title>

    <p>
      Just as Java allows both instance and static methods, fields can also be instance (object 
      data) or static (class data). The difference is critical: a field declared with the keyword 
      <c>static</c> belongs to the class, not an individual object. It is stored in the Static 
      memory segment (not the stack or the heap). A static field is shared among all objects of 
      that class. If one instance changes it, it changes for all instances.
    </p>

    <p>
      This can be the source of subtle bugs for beginning programmers. Let's look at an example. 
      Suppose in <c>Point</c> we had accidentally declared the <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> fields as 
      <c>static</c>:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
// Mistake! Do not declare instance fields static!    
private static double x;
private static double y;
    </program>

    <p>
      Technically, these fields should be referenced in the code as <c>Point.x</c> and <c>Point.y</c>, 
      not <c>this.x</c> and <c>this.y</c>. However, referencing them as <c>this.x</c> and 
      <c>this.y</c> is not a syntax error. Once you make the mistake of declaring instance fields 
      as <c>static</c>, everywhere you reference the fields with <c>this.</c>, Eclipse places 
      a warning symbol in the margin: <q>The static field Point.x should be accessed in a static way.</q> 
      The compiler is warning you that <c>x</c> is a static field, so accessing it through 
      <c>this.</c> is confusing and indirect<mdash/><c>x</c> is not part of the individual object 
      referenced by <c>this</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The code will still execute, however. Let's trace what happens when lines 5, 6, and 17 of 
      <c>PointDemo.main</c> execute:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p2 = new Point(5.0, 12.0);
p1.translate(3.0, 4.0); 
    </program>

    <p>
      Before line 5 executes, the Java Virtual Machine loads the <c>Point</c> class into the 
      Static memory segment. The static fields <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> are created and each is given 
      a default value of <c>0.0</c>. On line 5, memory is allocated on the heap for <c>p1</c>. 
      No space is needed inside the object for <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> because those are static 
      fields, though housekeeping information is stored (such as a link to the class blueprint).
    </p>

    <p>
      The constructor is invoked with the arguments <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c>. Inside the constructor:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
this.x = x;
this.y = y;
    </program>

    <p>
      <c>Point.x</c> (the shared static field) is assigned <c>3.0</c>, and <c>Point.y</c> is 
      assigned <c>4.0</c>. Execution returns to <c>PointDemo.main</c>, the object reference is 
      assigned to <c>p1</c>, and we proceed to line 6. <xref ref="fig-memory-static-p1"/> shows 
      the stack, heap, and static segment after line 5 executes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-memory-static-p1">
      <caption>Memory layout after line 5 executes with accidental static fields.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_4.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A three-column memory diagram showing Call Stack on the left, Heap in the center, 
          and Static Segment on the right. On the stack, p1 holds address 0x100. On the heap, 
          an empty Point object exists at address 0x100 containing class linkage but no instance 
          variables. In the Static Segment, the Point class blueprint contains shared static 
          fields x set to 3.0 and y set to 4.0.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      On line 6, we create another <c>Point</c> object, passing <c>5.0</c> and <c>12.0</c> to 
      the constructor. Memory is allocated on the heap for <c>p2</c>. Inside the constructor, 
      we assign <c>5.0</c> to <c>Point.x</c> and <c>12.0</c> to <c>Point.y</c>, overwriting the 
      <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c> that were previously there!
    </p>

    <p>
      Because static fields are shared, every time a constructor or mutator executes, the field 
      is overwritten for all objects. There is only one copy in memory. <xref ref="fig-memory-static-p2"/> 
      shows memory after line 6 executes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-memory-static-p2">
      <caption>Memory layout after line 6 executes: p2 overwrites the shared static fields.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_5.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A three-column memory diagram after p2 is created. On the stack, p1 references 0x100 
          and p2 references 0x200. On the heap, two Point objects exist, neither containing x 
          or y fields. In the Static Segment, the shared fields inside Point have been overwritten: 
          x is now 5.0 and y is now 12.0. Both p1 and p2 now see these overwritten values.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      On line 17, when <c>p1.translate(3.0, 4.0)</c> runs, it adds <c>3.0</c> to <c>Point.x</c> 
      (<m>5.0 + 3.0 = 8.0</m>) and <c>4.0</c> to <c>Point.y</c> (<m>12.0 + 4.0 = 16.0</m>), 
      because <c>p2</c> had already overwritten the original coordinates. Anything that happens 
      to either <c>p1</c> or <c>p2</c> changes the data for both. Invoking <c>getX()</c> and 
      <c>getY()</c> on either point will return the exact same coordinates. <xref ref="fig-memory-static-translated"/> 
      shows memory after line 17 executes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-memory-static-translated">
      <caption>Memory layout after line 17 executes: mutating p1 mutates the shared data for p2.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_6.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A three-column memory diagram after p1.translate(3.0, 4.0) runs. On the stack, p1 and 
          p2 remain unchanged. On the heap, both Point objects remain empty of instance fields. 
          In the Static Segment, shared fields x and y are updated to 8.0 and 16.0 respectively, 
          altering the state observed through both p1 and p2 simultaneously.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      If you encounter a bug where creating or modifying one object mysteriously alters the data 
      inside a completely different object, check your class definition immediately<mdash/>you have 
      almost certainly declared an instance field as <c>static</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-5-3-constants-counters">
    <title>Constants and Class Counters</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        So why does Java support static, class-level fields? Do they serve a useful purpose? Absolutely!
      </p>

      <p>
        Static fields are the right tool when data truly belongs to the entire class rather than an 
        individual instance. Two common uses include shared constants (like <c>Math.PI</c> or 
        conversion factors) and class-wide counters that track the total number of objects created.
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-5-3-1-constants">
      <title>Static Constants</title>

      <p>
        Suppose our EV dashboard wants to display an EV's data in metric units, even though it 
        is stored in imperial units. In the odometer calculation, we might use a conversion factor:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
final double KM_PER_MILE = 1.609344;
      </program>

      <p>
        Conversion factors are an excellent candidate for storage as static fields. Instead of 
        declaring <c>KM_PER_MILE</c> inside a single method, it can be shared across the entire 
        <c>ElectricVehicle</c> class. Static fields, by convention, are declared above instance 
        fields:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class ElectricVehicle {

    private static final double KM_PER_MILE = 1.609344;

    private String model;
    private double batteryCapacityKwh;
    private double milesPerKwh;
    ...
      </program>

      <p>
        Within the class code, <c>KM_PER_MILE</c> can be referenced by the class name dot field: 
        <c>ElectricVehicle.KM_PER_MILE</c>. Prepending the name with the class name is recommended 
        for clarity, so another programmer reading the code immediately recognizes that the 
        constant is defined at the class level.
      </p>

      <p>
        The advantage of a static constant is that it is stored once<mdash/>in the Static memory 
        segment for the <c>ElectricVehicle</c> class blueprint. If it were an instance field, every 
        single <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object on the heap would carry its own duplicate copy, 
        wasting memory. If it were local to a method, it would be allocated on the call stack 
        every time that method executes.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the constant is useful to other parts of the program (just as <c>Math.PI</c> is used 
        broadly across programs), it should be declared <c>public</c>. It then becomes part of 
        the class's public interface.
      </p>

      <p>
        Up until now, we have insisted that fields must always be <c>private</c> to protect data 
        integrity. A <c>public static final</c> constant is the one accepted exception to this 
        rule. Because the <c>final</c> keyword prevents anyone from ever reassigning the variable, 
        making a constant <c>public</c> exposes the useful value without any risk of data corruption.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-5-3-2-counters">
      <title>Static Object Counters</title>

      <p>
        A static field can also act as an accumulator that all objects contribute to. Suppose we 
        wanted to track how many <c>Point</c> objects have been created during program execution. 
        We can add a static counter field to the class, and have each constructor execution 
        increment that count:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class Point {

    private static int pointCount = 0;

    private double x;
    private double y;

    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
        Point.pointCount++;
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        We can also provide a public static accessor method so clients can inspect the count:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * Returns the number of Point objects created.
     * 
     * @return the number of Point objects created.
     */
    public static int getPointCount() {
        return Point.pointCount;
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        This method is declared <c>static</c> because <c>pointCount</c> is a static field. A client 
        programmer should not have to instantiate a <c>Point</c> object (which would increment 
        the counter!) simply to query how many points have already been created.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>pointCount</c> field resides in the Static memory location for <c>Point</c>, with 
        an initial value of <c>0</c>. Each time a <c>Point</c> object is created with <c>new</c>, 
        the constructor executes and increments <c>Point.pointCount</c>. If we ran this version of 
        <c>Point</c> in <c>PointDemo.main</c> and paused after creating two points, <c>Point.pointCount</c> 
        would equal <c>2</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you cannot see your static variables when running in the Eclipse debugger, click on the 
        three dots in the upper right of the Variables pane, choose <strong>Java</strong>, and select 
        <strong>Show Static Variables</strong>. You will see <c>pointCount</c> in each object's 
        memory area, marked with a small <c>S</c>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-debugger-static-var"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-debugger-static-var">
        <caption>Showing static variables in the Eclipse debugger.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_7.jpg" width="90%">
          <description>
            A screenshot of the Eclipse debugger Variables pane showing two Point instances. 
            Under each instance, static variables are displayed with a small red 'S' decorator icon, 
            showing pointCount equal to 2 shared identically across both variables.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-5-4-static-barrier">
    <title>The Static Barrier: Why Static Methods Cannot Access Instance Fields</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now that we have explored both instance and static members, a natural question arises about 
        how they interact. We have seen that an instance method can freely access a static field 
        (such as the <c>Point</c> constructor incrementing <c>Point.pointCount</c>). But can a 
        static method access an instance field or call an instance method?
      </p>

      <p>
        The relationship between the static world and the instance world is a one-way street. 
        An instance method has complete access to everything in the class: it can read instance 
        fields, modify instance fields, call other instance methods, read static constants, and 
        invoke static helper methods.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <c>static</c> method, however, hits a rigid architectural wall: <strong>a static method 
        can never directly access an instance variable or invoke an instance method without an 
        explicit object reference</strong>.
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-5-4-1-which-object">
      <title>Which Object Would <c>this</c> Be?</title>

      <p>
        Recall from Section 6.3 that whenever an instance method executes, Java implicitly passes 
        a reference to the specific object sitting before the dot. That reference is stored in 
        the <c>this</c> keyword:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When you invoke <c>p1.distanceFromOrigin()</c>, <c>this</c> points to <c>p1</c>'s 
            object on the heap.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When you invoke <c>p2.distanceFromOrigin()</c>, <c>this</c> points to <c>p2</c>'s 
            object on the heap.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Instance methods can freely reference <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> because <c>this.x</c> and 
        <c>this.y</c> always have a concrete target in memory.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <c>static</c> method, by contrast, is invoked on the class itself (<c>Point.getPointCount()</c>), 
        not on an object. Because no object was used to call the method, a static method has no 
        <c>this</c> reference. There is no implicit heap address passed into the call.
      </p>

      <p>
        Imagine what would happen if Java allowed a static method to read an instance variable directly:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class Point {
    private double x; // Instance variable (one per object on the heap)
    private double y; // Instance variable (one per object on the heap)

    // COMPILER ERROR: Attempting to read instance fields from a static method!
    public static void printCoordinates() {
        System.out.println("(" + x + ", " + y + ")"); 
    }
}
      </program>

      <p>
        If you call <c>Point.printCoordinates()</c> from <c>main</c>, the compiler faces an 
        impossible question: <em>whose <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> should it print?</em>
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If our program has instantiated five different <c>Point</c> objects on the heap, 
            which of the five sets of coordinates should Java choose?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What if our program has not created any <c>Point</c> objects yet? Where would <c>x</c> 
            and <c>y</c> come from?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Because a static method has no <c>this</c> pointer targeting a specific instance on the 
        heap, referencing an instance variable inside a static method is logically undefined. 
        If you attempt to write code like the example above, the Java compiler immediately halts 
        with a diagnostic error:
      </p>

      <console>
        <output>
Cannot make a static reference to the non-static field x.
        </output>
      </console>

      <p>
        The compiler is warning you that you are inside a static method belonging to the class as 
        a whole. You are trying to use an instance member (<c>x</c>), but you have not told the 
        compiler which specific object on the heap to inspect.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-5-4-2-static-using-objects">
      <title>A Static Method Can Use an Object</title>

      <p>
        Does this mean a static method can never interact with an object? Not at all! A static 
        method simply cannot access an instance member <em>implicitly</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If a static method needs to work with instance data, it must be given an <em>explicit 
        object reference</em><mdash/>either passed in as a parameter or instantiated locally 
        with <c>new</c><mdash/>and access that data using the dot operator:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
// LEGAL: The static method is explicitly given an object to examine
public static void printPoint(Point p) {
    // Java knows exactly whose coordinates to read: p's!
    System.out.println("(" + p.getX() + ", " + p.getY() + ")");
}
      </program>

      <p>
        Here, <c>printPoint</c> is a static helper method. It does not possess an implicit 
        <c>this</c> reference, but because we explicitly passed it the parameter <c>p</c>, 
        the compiler knows precisely which heap object to read.
      </p>

      <p>
        To summarize:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Inside an instance method:</strong> You are standing inside an object on the 
            heap. You can see your own instance fields (<c>this.x</c>), other instance methods, 
            and all shared class-level static members.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Inside a static method:</strong> You are standing outside on the class 
            blueprint. You can see shared static fields and call other static methods, but you 
            cannot see inside any individual heap object unless you hold an explicit reference 
            to that object.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>